<BaseLayout
  title="Office Pest Control | Permian Basin"
  description="Break rooms, door thresholds and empty buildings — the three things that actually drive office pest problems, plus the workplace notice rule that binds you."
  path="/commercial/offices/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Commercial · offices</p>
      <h1>Offices</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Offices are the easiest commercial category to service and the one where customers are most
          surprised to need anybody at all. There is no food production, no stored commodity, no inbound
          freight, no public throughput.
        </p>
        <p>
          What there is, is a break room, a set of doors, and a building that nobody is in for two-thirds
          of the week. Those three things account for almost every office pest problem we deal with.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The break room is the whole problem</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          In a premises that handles no food commercially, the break room is frequently the only food
          source in the building — which makes it not a contributing factor but the entire explanation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It is also the most under-managed room in most offices</strong>, because it belongs to
          everybody and therefore to nobody. Cleaning contracts often cover floors and surfaces and stop
          short of the inside of a microwave, the seal of a fridge door, or the underside of a toaster.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Where the problems actually are.</strong> Under and behind the fridge, which is warm,
          dark and undisturbed — and warmth matters, because German cockroach development is optimal at
          around eighty degrees. Behind and inside the microwave. The seams of upholstered seating. The gap
          between the counter and the wall. Food stored in desks and lockers around the building rather
          than in the kitchen at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And the moisture.</strong> A sink nobody claims, a dripping tap, a fridge drip tray, a
          water cooler with a wet mat under it. In a semi-arid climate any reliable moisture indoors is
          worth more to a pest than any food source, and it is usually easier to fix.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What actually works, and it is not a treatment.</strong> Make the break room somebody's
          explicit responsibility. Put food storage in sealed containers rather than in desk drawers. Empty
          the bin at end of day rather than end of week. Clean under and behind the appliances on a
          schedule that exists in writing. On a great many offices that alone resolves the problem, and we
          will tell you so rather than sell you a quarterly programme against a fridge nobody moves.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The doors, and the empty hours</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Everything gets in through something.</strong> Exterior doors and their sweeps, service
          and back doors, utility penetrations, vents, and where the wall meets the slab. The exclusion
          standard is a quarter of an inch — AgriLife describes mice getting through an opening the size of
          a pencil diameter — and extension guidance is to seal everything larger than that regardless of
          which species you think you have.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Seal with metal or concrete.</strong> Foam, plastic, wood and rubber are all gnawable and
          are not a permanent rodent seal, whatever a contractor tells you.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Doors propped open matter more than people think.</strong> In this climate a door held
          open for deliveries, for a smoke break, or simply because the entry system is awkward is a real
          and repeated opening, and it is usually the one nobody counts.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then the empty hours.</strong> An office is unoccupied roughly two-thirds of the week,
          and completely still for long stretches. Nothing about that creates pests — but it means activity
          runs unobserved. Something that would be noticed within a day in an occupied home has a weekend,
          and over a holiday period it has a fortnight.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The drains dry out.</strong> Which is the second consequence of a quiet building, and it
          is the one nobody anticipates. A floor drain or a rarely-used washroom trap that dries is an open
          pipe from the sewer, and AgriLife's prevention guidance is verbatim: keep P-traps filled with
          water to create a barrier between the sewer and the building. American and oriental cockroaches
          use floor drains as common points of entry. Running water through the seldom-used fixtures weekly
          costs nothing and closes the route.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And an empty building is a cool building</strong>, or a hot one, depending on how the
          system is set back. Either way it changes the gradient at every door and gap — which, as the next
          section covers, is precisely what a scorpion is looking for.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Scorpions in ground-floor offices</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          This is the regional item that surprises people who have moved into the area, and it applies to
          any suite with an exterior door.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The mechanism is temperature and moisture.</strong> AgriLife's E-362 notes that during
          hot weather scorpions may move into living areas to escape high temperatures in attics, and
          AgriLife extension entomologists describe them seeking water and cooler conditions in hot dry
          weather — squeezing in where they feel a temperature difference below a door or through another
          small crack.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Which describes an air-conditioned office building precisely.</strong> A cooled interior
          against a hundred-degree exterior is the largest temperature gradient on the property, and it is
          strongest at exactly the thresholds where the seals are worst.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>So the pattern is: enter low, live high.</strong> In under a door or through a
          penetration, then up into wall voids and ceiling spaces, then down into occupied space when the
          void overheats — which is why sightings cluster in July and August.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The fixes are mundane and effective.</strong> Door sweeps first, which is the single
          highest-value item. Then weep hole screening, sealing around penetrations, and keeping
          landscaping, stored material and rock features back from the building. AgriLife's own management
          guidance leads with harbourage removal rather than with chemistry, and on scorpion work that
          ranking is not close.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And it is worth saying plainly to staff:</strong> no scorpion in Texas is considered
          deadly, per AgriLife, and stings produce only moderate reactions in most people. They hurt, local
          swelling can last days, and anyone with a known allergy should take it seriously — but the fear
          in a workplace is usually out of proportion to the animal, and telling people that is part of
          managing the problem.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Occupied space and the people in it</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Offices are the category where treatment happens closest to people who did not choose to be
          there, and that deserves more thought than it usually gets.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Tell people in advance.</strong> The posted sign is the legal minimum; a note to staff is
          the sensible version. Most objections to workplace pest control are objections to being
          surprised.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Flag sensitivities before the visit.</strong> Asthma, respiratory conditions, pregnancy,
          chemical sensitivities. Also aquariums, which are common in offices and genuinely matter, and any
          plants that are somebody's project. All of those change what is used and where, and every one of
          them is easier to plan around than to work around.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The pest is part of the health picture too.</strong> AgriLife specifies HEPA vacuuming
          for cockroach work because harbourages contain allergens, and the National Institute of
          Environmental Health Sciences notes a link between cockroach presence and increased severity of
          asthma symptoms. So an untreated cockroach problem in an occupied office is not a neutral option
          — it is a trade-off, and it is worth framing it that way in a conversation with staff rather than
          pretending the only risk is the treatment.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And be careful about who authorises what.</strong> In an office the person who books the
          work, the person who signs for it, and the people who will be standing next to it are frequently
          three different groups. Somebody should be telling the third group, and it should not be the
          first they hear of it when a technician arrives.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Out of hours, generally.</strong> Better result, fewer complications, simpler notice
          mechanics, and nobody working next to it. It is what we recommend for almost every office job.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And targeted rather than broadcast.</strong> Crevice and harbourage placement rather than
          general application across occupied space. That is better practice regardless, and in an office
          it also happens to be what people would want if they knew to ask.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The other things offices actually call about</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Beyond the break room and the doors, a short list covers nearly everything else.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Ants on a moisture trail.</strong> Usually following a water source rather than a food
          one — a break room sink, a leaking fitting, a condensate line, a planter that gets over-watered.
          Following the trail back to where it enters the wall is more productive than treating where the
          ants are visible, and the moisture fix is frequently the whole answer.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Spiders in corners and store rooms.</strong> Mostly harmless and mostly a symptom: a
          building with spiders is a building with enough insects to feed them. Reducing the insects and
          the harbourage does more than treating the webs.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Wasps at an entrance.</strong> The one office situation that is genuinely urgent, because
          it is at a door everybody has to use and because somebody in the building may be allergic. Nests
          under eaves, over doorways and in signage are the usual sites. This is also the situation the
          emergency provision in the rules was written for.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Crickets in autumn.</strong> A West Texas seasonal event that reaches ground-floor
          offices through the same door gaps as everything else, and which is made dramatically worse by
          exterior lighting. Lights on all night beside a glass entrance concentrate insects at exactly the
          point where the seals are weakest.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Rodents in a suspended ceiling.</strong> Heard rather than seen, usually at a consistent
          hour. Rodents travel along edges — thigmotaxis, in AgriLife's term: linear pathways, wall and
          floor corners, structural runs. Devices go on those runs, close to the wall and no more than
          about ten feet apart in an active area, rather than scattered across the void.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And something dead in a wall.</strong> Which is not a treatment problem but is very much
          an office problem, and it is worth saying that the usual cause is somebody having put out bait
          without exclusion. Trapping rather than baiting in an occupied building avoids it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Multi-tenant buildings</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Most offices in this region are one suite in a shared building, which means part of your pest
          picture belongs to somebody else.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Find out where the line is.</strong> Many leases put common areas on the landlord and
          suite interiors on the tenant. That is workable, but it produces a gap precisely where problems
          travel — shared voids, plumbing chases, roof spaces, service corridors and the bin area.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>German cockroaches move between suites.</strong> They do not live outdoors in Texas, so
          a population in your suite was carried in — possibly by you, in a delivery or somebody's
          groceries, and just as possibly from the tenant next door. Treating one suite while the adjacent
          one is untreated is frequently spending against continuous re-supply.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Rodents use the shared roof void.</strong> Roof rats nest above ground — attics, wall
          voids, ceiling voids — and forage up to three hundred feet, which in a shared building covers
          every suite in it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The shared bin area serves everybody equally</strong>, including whichever tenant is not
          managing theirs.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>So the useful first step is a conversation with the building manager</strong> rather than
          a treatment. If there is a building-wide programme, joining it beats running a parallel one. If
          there is not, and the problem is structural, saying so to the person who can authorise the work
          is worth more than treating your own carpet.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>We will tell you when that is the situation</strong>, even though it means a smaller job
          or none at all. A tenant paying to treat a suite that is being re-supplied from next door is
          paying for a subscription rather than a solution.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Notice, records and how it runs</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>You are a workplace.</strong> Rule 4 TAC §7.146(b) requires us to provide the pest
          control sign to the employer or building manager at least forty-eight hours before a planned
          indoor treatment, and §7.147(d) adds the consumer information sheet. Under §7.148(b) you post it
          in an area of common access that employees are most likely to see, at least forty-eight hours
          ahead, and make the sheet available on request during normal business hours.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Two reliefs.</strong> Section 7.146(d): no violation where the space to be treated is
          vacant, unused and unoccupied. Section 7.148(e): no violation if the sign is removed by an
          unauthorised person. Both are useful in a building where suites empty and notices come down.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And the emergency provision, used properly.</strong> Section 7.147(g) waives
          pre-notification where the customer and the certified applicator sign a statement attesting to an
          emergency requiring immediate treatment — an imminent hazard to health, with treatment limited to
          the localised area, and the signed statement kept with the use records. It exists for a situation like a wasp nest
          over a door that everybody has to walk under, and it is not a way of absorbing a scheduling
          squeeze.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Records for two years</strong> under §7.144, including pesticides with EPA registration
          numbers, amounts, mixing rates, target pest, dates and applicator details. Invoices and contracts
          carrying the business name, licence number, phone and address under §7.145, with the jurisdiction
          statement on contracts. A certificate of insurance naming your entity on request — managed office
          buildings usually want one on file before a vendor comes on site.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>No contracts.</strong> Bi-monthly or quarterly, no term, no cancellation fee, interim
          visits included. And on many offices the honest recommendation is not a recurring programme at
          all — it is a single visit, a short exclusion list and an honest conversation about the break room. If that is what your
          building needs, that is what you will be told.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqs} heading="Office pest control questions" />
  <Cta
    line="Tell him what was seen and where, and whether you are a whole building or one suite in a shared one. On offices that second answer often changes the recommendation entirely."
  />
</BaseLayout>
